            </sidebyside>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the following and prove.
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>\chi (K_n)</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>\chi (K_{m,n})</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>\chi (P_n)</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>\chi (C_n)</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              What are all possible things that can happen to the chromatic number of a graph if you add an edge to the graph without adding any vertices?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>G</m> be a graph that has a cycle of odd order.  What is the smallest <m>\chi(G)</m> can be?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      Section 5.4 (Keller &amp; Trotter): pp. 80-84
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>